* Numbers that can be below zero.
*
* A cell counts upwards from nothing and has nowhere to put a minus sign, so a
* signed value here is two cells: a `sign`, which is 1 when the value is
* negative and 0 when it is not, and a `size`, which is how far from zero it
* is. A program says `@signed_add(x_sign, x_size, c_sign, c_size)` and never
* has to remember which is which.
*
* Sign and size rather than the wrapping arithmetic a machine uses. The reason
* is multiplication: multiplying two wrapped numbers means pulling the sign
* back out of each of them first, which costs a comparison against 128 every
* time, where multiplying these is multiplying the sizes and asking whether
* the signs agree. Addition pays for that -- one case for signs that agree and
* two for signs that face off -- and addition is the cheaper thing to make
* awkward.
*
* **A size is one cell, so it runs from 0 to 255, and nothing here says so at
* run time.** `-200 + -56` is 256, which wraps to a size of zero and is then
* handed back as a well-formed positive zero -- the one value this file
* promises to normalise is the one an overflow forges. Keep sizes well inside
* the range; a program that cannot needs two-cell sizes, which is
* `lib/wide.bfm`'s business.
*
* Zero is written with a positive sign, at every door: `@signed_put` refuses to
* write a negative zero and `@signed_add` normalises one away. Without the
* second, two sizes that cancel leave the sign of whichever was written first,
* so `-4 + 4` came out as a zero that compared unequal to zero.
*
* **This file owns cells 20 to 45**, the way `lib/fast.bfm` owns 0 to 19. The
* first draft passed the scratch in and `@signed_add` took fourteen
* parameters, eleven of them cells nobody wanted to name. Two `at`s naming one
* cell are allowed on purpose, so nothing will tell you if a program pins one
* of these; pin nothing below 46 in a program that includes this file.

@include "idioms.bfm"
@include "wide.bfm"

@var s_agree at 20
@var s_smaller at 21
@var s_keep at 22
@var s_bsign at 23
@var s_bsize at 24
@var s_walk at 25
@var s_step at 26
@var s_passed at 27
@var s_flag at 28
@var s_other at 29
@var s_scratch at 30
@var s_spare at 31
@var s_sign at 32
@var s_size at 33
@var s_guard at 34
@var s_inner at 35
@var s_hi at 36
@var s_lo at 37
@var s_count at 38
@var s_bit at 39
@var s_half at 40
@var s_carry at 41
@var s_held at 42
@var s_t0 at 43
@var s_zero at 44
@var s_out at 45

* A signed value from a sign and a size written down. A size of nothing is
* positive whatever was asked for, because that is the only zero this file
* knows how to compare.
*
*     sign, size = (0 if m == 0 else s), m
@macro signed_put(sign, size, s, m) {
    @put(sign, s)
    @put(size, m)
    @unless(size, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @clear(sign)
    }
}

* The same value the other way up. Zero has no other way up, so it is left
* alone rather than made negative.
*
*     if size:
*         sign = 1 - sign
@macro signed_negate(sign, size) {
    @when(size, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @unless(sign, s_inner, s_scratch, s_spare) {
            @put(s_keep, 1)
        }
        @clear(sign)
        @move(s_keep, sign)
    }
}

* `a` gains `b`; `b` keeps its value.
*
*     if a_sign == b_sign:          # the same way: the sizes add
*         a_size += b_size
*     elif a_size < b_size:         # facing off, and b wins
*         a_sign, a_size = b_sign, b_size - a_size
*     else:                         # facing off, and a wins
*         a_size -= b_size
*     if a_size == 0:
*         a_sign = 0
*
* Everything is done against a *copy* of `b`, which costs two cells and buys
* `@signed_add(x_sign, x_size, x_sign, x_size)` -- doubling a value, an
* ordinary thing to want. Without the copy that spins for ever: `@copy` moves
* a cell into another one and back, and with one cell for both ends the count
* never comes down.
@macro signed_add(a_sign, a_size, b_sign, b_size) {
    @clear(s_bsign)
    @copy(b_sign, s_bsign, s_spare)
    @clear(s_bsize)
    @copy(b_size, s_bsize, s_spare)

    @clear(s_agree)
    @copy(a_sign, s_agree, s_spare)
    @equal(s_agree, s_bsign, s_scratch, s_spare)

    @when(s_agree, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @copy(s_bsize, a_size, s_spare)
    }

    @unless(s_agree, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @less(a_size, s_bsize, s_smaller, s_walk, s_step, s_passed, s_flag, s_other, s_scratch, s_spare)

        @when(s_smaller, s_inner, s_scratch, s_spare) {
            * b is the larger, so the answer faces b's way and is b - a.
            @clear(s_keep)
            @copy(s_bsize, s_keep, s_spare)
            @to a_size
            [
                -
                @to s_keep
                -
                @to a_size
            ]
            @move(s_keep, a_size)
            @clear(a_sign)
            @copy(s_bsign, a_sign, s_spare)
        }

        @unless(s_smaller, s_inner, s_scratch, s_spare) {
            * a is at least as large, so the answer keeps a's sign.
            @clear(s_keep)
            @copy(s_bsize, s_keep, s_spare)
            @to s_keep
            [
                -
                @to a_size
                -
                @to s_keep
            ]
        }
    }

    * Nothing is negative. Two sizes that cancel would otherwise leave the
    * sign of whichever was written first.
    @unless(a_size, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @clear(a_sign)
    }
}

* `a` loses `b`; `b` keeps its value. Adding it the other way up, on a copy.
@macro signed_sub(a_sign, a_size, b_sign, b_size) {
    @clear(s_sign)
    @copy(b_sign, s_sign, s_spare)
    @clear(s_size)
    @copy(b_size, s_size, s_spare)
    @signed_negate(s_sign, s_size)
    @signed_add(a_sign, a_size, s_sign, s_size)
}

* Half of a cell, taken one pair at a time.
*
*     out = cell // 2          # and `cell` is emptied
*
* Take one, and look for a second: two taken together are one half. What is
* taken alone is the remainder, which nothing here wants and everything here
* has to account for.
*
* Asking `cell` whether anything is left copies it, which costs its value --
* so this is quadratic, and the four halvings that scale a product are a large
* share of a multiply. The obvious fix is a single bit flipped once a step,
* since asking a bit costs nothing; I wrote it, and it came out *slower* --
* 34,644 steps against 31,529 on a 40 by 40 product -- because four macro
* calls a turn move the cursor further than the copy they replace. Left as it
* is, with the measurement, rather than made worse for a good reason.
@macro signed_halve(cell, out) {
    @clear(out)
    @clear(s_bit)
    @while(cell) {
        @to cell
        -
        @to s_bit
        +
        @when(cell, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
            @to cell
            -
            @to s_bit
            -
            @to out
            +
        }
    }
    @clear(s_bit)
}

* One signed value onto another; the source keeps its value.
*
*     to_sign, to_size = from_sign, from_size
@macro signed_copy(from_sign, from_size, to_sign, to_size) {
    @clear(to_sign)
    @copy(from_sign, to_sign, s_spare)
    @clear(to_size)
    @copy(from_size, to_size, s_spare)
}

* `a` becomes `a` times `b`, at the scale a fixed-point number is kept at;
* `b` keeps its value.
*
*     size = (a_size * b_size) >> FRACTION_BITS
*     sign = (a_sign != b_sign)
*
* This is the whole reason the sign is a cell of its own: the sizes multiply
* and the signs are compared, where two wrapped numbers would each have to
* have a sign pulled back out of them first.
*
* The product is two cells because 64 times 64 does not fit in one, and the
* four shifts that bring it back down are four halvings of the low half plus
* sixteen times the high one -- `(hi*256 + lo) >> 4` is `hi*16 + lo>>4`.
*
* **The answer has to fit a cell, so the product must stay under 4096**: two
* sizes of 63 are the largest square that works, and 64 times 64 comes back as
* 0 rather than as the 256 that does not fit. Nothing says so at run time,
* because a cell wraps rather than complaining. A first draft of this comment
* said "below about 90", which is wrong by half and was wrong in the same
* paragraph that says 64 times 64 does not fit.
*
* Counting `a` times costs the value of `a`, and doubling and adding instead
* would cost its digits. That was built and measured and came out 2.5 times
* slower on `mandelbrot.bfm`, which multiplies small numbers and zeros; it
* would still win on operands near the 4096 limit. See `docs/performance.md`.
@macro signed_multiply(a_sign, a_size, b_sign, b_size) {
    * A product faces the way its parts disagree.
    @clear(s_agree)
    @copy(a_sign, s_agree, s_spare)
    @equal(s_agree, b_sign, s_scratch, s_spare)
    @clear(a_sign)
    @unless(s_agree, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @put(a_sign, 1)
    }

    * The product, in two cells: `b` added to nothing, `a` times over.
    @clear(s_hi)
    @clear(s_lo)
    @clear(s_count)
    @copy(a_size, s_count, s_spare)
    @while(s_count) {
        @clear(s_t0)
        @copy(b_size, s_t0, s_spare)
        @while(s_t0) {
            @wide_inc(s_hi, s_lo, s_carry, s_held)
            @to s_t0
            -
        }
        @to s_count
        -
    }

    * Down four bits: the low half halved four times, and the high half worth
    * sixteen of what is left.
    @signed_halve(s_lo, s_half)
    @signed_halve(s_half, s_lo)
    @signed_halve(s_lo, s_half)
    @signed_halve(s_half, s_lo)

    * The multiplicand is not wanted any more, and the answer goes in its
    * place. Emptied here rather than earlier, because the product loop above
    * counts it down.
    @clear(a_size)
    @copy(s_lo, a_size, s_spare)

    * Sixteen of the high half, moved rather than multiplied: the general
    * `@multiply` copies its multiplier once per unit, and this is a run the
    * optimizer folds into one step.
    @to s_hi
    [
        -
        @to a_size
        +{16}
        @to s_hi
    ]

    @unless(a_size, s_guard, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @clear(a_sign)
    }
}
